WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.020 --> 00:00:00.853
(audience cheers and applauds)

2
00:00:00.853 --> 00:00:02.233
Hi buddy, how are ya?

3
00:00:03.530 --> 00:00:06.450
I have our cup, we are serving the angels.

4
00:00:06.450 --> 00:00:07.370
Oh my gosh.
Oh!

5
00:00:07.370 --> 00:00:10.190
Gosh, I don't have my cup, Rachael, I'm such a goober.

6
00:00:10.190 --> 00:00:12.070
Ugh, it's somewhere.

7
00:00:12.070 --> 00:00:14.810
I'll find it, maybe in the commercial break.

8
00:00:14.810 --> 00:00:15.643
Guess what I was-

9
00:00:15.643 --> 00:00:17.510
Oh my God, please, put that down, look at that haircut.

10
00:00:17.510 --> 00:00:18.343
Put it down.
(audience laughs)

11
00:00:18.343 --> 00:00:20.260
No, I don't mean to be creepy,

12
00:00:20.260 --> 00:00:22.570
but this is what I was on my couch with,

13
00:00:22.570 --> 00:00:24.100
and then eventually in bed with.

14
00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:28.240
I couldn't put it down, and I just read it front to back,

15
00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:32.670
it's all dogeared, and what I loved about this cookbook,

16
00:00:32.670 --> 00:00:34.120
reading it last night, Rachael,

17
00:00:34.120 --> 00:00:37.840
was how much I feel like no one is like you.

18
00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:41.180
You talk about grabbing a handful as a measurement,

19
00:00:41.180 --> 00:00:43.330
taking the stress out.

20
00:00:43.330 --> 00:00:46.900
I think you're one of the most incredibly liberating,

21
00:00:46.900 --> 00:00:50.910
and encouraging people to be freer in the kitchen.

22
00:00:50.910 --> 00:00:54.360
You know, I never thought I would write a cookbook,

23
00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:55.890
or be on television ever.

24
00:00:55.890 --> 00:00:59.250
So, when I approached writing, it was always

25
00:00:59.250 --> 00:01:02.700
from the point of view of the home cook,

26
00:01:02.700 --> 00:01:06.100
not of the chef, or professional,

27
00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:09.700
or aspiring to be anything other than hungry,

28
00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:11.310
and making dinner.

29
00:01:11.310 --> 00:01:15.230
I still write in the same manner, 26 books later.

30
00:01:15.230 --> 00:01:18.530
And now, they're more like memoir mashups.

31
00:01:18.530 --> 00:01:22.190
I just finished my 27th and delivered it in December.

32
00:01:22.190 --> 00:01:24.300
It will be out next September.

33
00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:27.720
And it's mostly essays and some recipes,

34
00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:29.290
but I still write in the same manner

35
00:01:29.290 --> 00:01:31.960
that I did all those years ago.

36
00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:34.260
You have had such an incredible journey,

37
00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:37.440
and you have accomplished so much in your life.

38
00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:39.910
And I was wondering if you would take a trip

39
00:01:39.910 --> 00:01:41.560
down memory lane with me,

40
00:01:41.560 --> 00:01:44.440
and look at some milestones and memories.

41
00:01:44.440 --> 00:01:46.410
I'm good with anything for you, buddy.

42
00:01:46.410 --> 00:01:50.079
Okay, Let's take a look at Rachael Drew the Years.

43
00:01:50.079 --> 00:01:52.290
(audience applauding)

44
00:01:52.290 --> 00:01:54.003
Oh my gosh.

45
00:01:54.003 --> 00:01:59.003
All right, 1968, Rachael was born on August 25th.

46
00:01:59.030 --> 00:02:01.470
What are some of your very first memories

47
00:02:01.470 --> 00:02:02.850
when you were a kid?

48
00:02:02.850 --> 00:02:06.000
My very first memory is my mom

49
00:02:07.230 --> 00:02:10.690 line:15%
in the kitchen at the restaurant.

50
00:02:10.690 --> 00:02:13.000 line:15%
Our restaurant was called The Carvery,

51
00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:15.880
and we were out in Cape Cod.

52
00:02:15.880 --> 00:02:19.220
And I remember being on my mom's hip and she was fighting

53
00:02:19.220 --> 00:02:22.620
with a purveyor on the phone, while she was heating

54
00:02:22.620 --> 00:02:25.390
up all of the griddles and grills and cooktops,

55
00:02:25.390 --> 00:02:27.750
and you know, she was turning everything on

56
00:02:27.750 --> 00:02:28.670
in the restaurant.

57
00:02:28.670 --> 00:02:31.590
And she went to hang up the phone, she put me down,

58
00:02:31.590 --> 00:02:36.130
and I put my hand up to grab a spatula,

59
00:02:36.130 --> 00:02:39.520
and I grilled my entire thumb

60
00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:42.962
from here to here to the stove top.

61
00:02:42.962 --> 00:02:43.795
(Drew shrieks)

62
00:02:43.795 --> 00:02:47.770
And today, it's a darker color than the rest of my hand.

63
00:02:47.770 --> 00:02:50.310
And that is literally my first memory in life,

64
00:02:50.310 --> 00:02:52.470
griddling myself.

65
00:02:52.470 --> 00:02:55.030
So it's kind of a "Harry Potter"-ish thing.

66
00:02:55.030 --> 00:02:55.863
You know what I mean?
(audience laughing)

67
00:02:55.863 --> 00:03:00.863
It's like, that certainly said what was born to be,

68
00:03:00.920 --> 00:03:05.180
and what I've done with the whole rest of my 52 years.

69
00:03:05.180 --> 00:03:07.940
I love that story so much.

70
00:03:07.940 --> 00:03:10.383
Let's see which year we're going to next!

71
00:03:11.310 --> 00:03:12.143
Oh gosh.

72
00:03:12.143 --> 00:03:14.660
1982, You started working as a bus girl,

73
00:03:14.660 --> 00:03:16.360
dishwasher, and fountain girl.

74
00:03:16.360 --> 00:03:17.760
What did those jobs teach you,

75
00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:20.070
and prepare you for the years to come?

76
00:03:20.070 --> 00:03:23.540
Dish machine operator, not a very glamorous life.

77
00:03:23.540 --> 00:03:26.027
A DMO, I was the DMO.

78
00:03:27.050 --> 00:03:29.890
And when I got promoted to the fountain,

79
00:03:29.890 --> 00:03:32.590
I thought my life would be amazing.

80
00:03:32.590 --> 00:03:34.120
It was like "Cinderella,"

81
00:03:34.120 --> 00:03:37.590
like, it was like being a prom queen,

82
00:03:37.590 --> 00:03:41.540
to go to the fountain and get to wear a dress,

83
00:03:41.540 --> 00:03:44.470
and waitress shoes instead of being

84
00:03:44.470 --> 00:03:47.880
at this 120-degree dish machine,

85
00:03:47.880 --> 00:03:52.130
buried in 3,000 dishes that smell like, you know,

86
00:03:52.130 --> 00:03:55.930
eggs and hamburgers, it was just so exciting.

87
00:03:55.930 --> 00:03:59.330
And then I realized that I was too short

88
00:03:59.330 --> 00:04:03.150
to scoop the ice cream, and there was a little faucet

89
00:04:03.150 --> 00:04:04.900
that would clean the scoops.

90
00:04:04.900 --> 00:04:09.480
So it was a constant stream of leftover ice cream

91
00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:12.210
on the side of the ice cream cooler.

92
00:04:12.210 --> 00:04:14.620
So when I pushed myself up and throw myself,

93
00:04:14.620 --> 00:04:18.760
hurl myself into the ice cream cooler to get the ice cream,

94
00:04:18.760 --> 00:04:23.250
I ended up with a line of melted, sour,

95
00:04:23.250 --> 00:04:26.660
every flavor that Howard Johnson served

96
00:04:26.660 --> 00:04:28.530
of ice cream across my boobs.
(audience laughs)

97
00:04:28.530 --> 00:04:32.391
So I became the girl with the ice cream boobs.

98
00:04:32.391 --> 00:04:33.224
(audience laughs)

99
00:04:33.224 --> 00:04:36.010
Not only was it worse than being at the dish machine,

100
00:04:36.010 --> 00:04:40.520
but I was out in public, and I had a line across myself,

101
00:04:40.520 --> 00:04:41.820
and I smelled.

102
00:04:41.820 --> 00:04:45.368
So, thanks for bringing up some fun times.

103
00:04:45.368 --> 00:04:46.201
(audience laughs)

104
00:04:46.201 --> 00:04:49.210
We said last night, it was like you had two scoops and-

105
00:04:49.210 --> 00:04:51.300
Yes, double, yes.
(audience laughs)

106
00:04:51.300 --> 00:04:53.620
I also didn't anticipate, Rachael,

107
00:04:53.620 --> 00:04:55.800
that I would be dressed like a soda jerk today,

108
00:04:55.800 --> 00:04:56.841
so this is perfect.
(audience laughs)

109
00:04:56.841 --> 00:04:57.674
(Rachael shrieks)

110
00:04:57.674 --> 00:04:58.980
All right, let's see what's next.

111
00:04:58.980 --> 00:05:00.190
You so are!

112
00:05:00.190 --> 00:05:01.830
I really, I mean, okay.

113
00:05:01.830 --> 00:05:03.940
1985!

114
00:05:03.940 --> 00:05:06.850
You started your first business, called Delicious Liaisons,

115
00:05:06.850 --> 00:05:08.100
while you were in high school.

116
00:05:08.100 --> 00:05:09.817
What was it?

117
00:05:09.817 --> 00:05:11.890
"Dangerous Liaisons" was a big movie,

118
00:05:11.890 --> 00:05:14.360
a big, big stage production, big movie,

119
00:05:14.360 --> 00:05:17.180
and great, great book and whatever.

120
00:05:17.180 --> 00:05:20.240
So I thought Delicious Liaisons, what a fun name.

121
00:05:20.240 --> 00:05:24.110
So I, just for fun, I made a fake catalog

122
00:05:25.500 --> 00:05:30.350
of Delicious Liaisons gift baskets for people. (laughs)

123
00:05:30.350 --> 00:05:32.290
And I showed it to my mother,

124
00:05:32.290 --> 00:05:34.520
and she thought it was so delightful,

125
00:05:34.520 --> 00:05:37.130
she took it down to the print shop,

126
00:05:37.130 --> 00:05:40.380
and she had a bunch of these made up.

127
00:05:40.380 --> 00:05:43.090
So I just started handing them out to random people

128
00:05:43.090 --> 00:05:45.600
at the grocery store, at the pizza place,

129
00:05:45.600 --> 00:05:48.190
in my class, give this to your mom or your dad.

130
00:05:48.190 --> 00:05:52.140
And I started making baskets for sale.

131
00:05:52.140 --> 00:05:54.660
It was just really an art project, but-

132
00:05:54.660 --> 00:05:59.170
Well, and you know, the origin story of a pioneer.

133
00:05:59.170 --> 00:06:03.480
Next, we are going to the year,

134
00:06:03.480 --> 00:06:07.320
1997, you launched 30-Minute Meals.

135
00:06:07.320 --> 00:06:09.200
(audience applauds and cheers)
Oh my gosh.

136
00:06:09.200 --> 00:06:11.390
With local news station-

137
00:06:11.390 --> 00:06:12.563
Yes, WRGB!

138
00:06:13.947 --> 00:06:16.480
Oh, WRGB!

139
00:06:16.480 --> 00:06:18.970
And you were in upstate New York.

140
00:06:18.970 --> 00:06:22.143
What was the initial launch like?

141
00:06:22.143 --> 00:06:25.380
My boss, who's one of my best friends to this day,

142
00:06:25.380 --> 00:06:29.770
Donna Carnevale, we are, her birthday's this week,

143
00:06:29.770 --> 00:06:30.950
by the way, happy birthday, Donna.

144
00:06:30.950 --> 00:06:31.950
Happy birthday!

145
00:06:31.950 --> 00:06:35.560
Yeah, we are super dear friends to this day.

146
00:06:35.560 --> 00:06:38.560
Donna said, people love your food.

147
00:06:38.560 --> 00:06:40.640
Why don't you just teach the class?

148
00:06:40.640 --> 00:06:43.790
We had an idea to sell cooking classes,

149
00:06:43.790 --> 00:06:48.520 line:15%
and we made the mistake, as to real savvy business ladies,

150
00:06:48.520 --> 00:06:51.270 line:15%
to under-price the classes.

151
00:06:51.270 --> 00:06:54.280
So when we went to ask chefs to teach the classes

152
00:06:54.280 --> 00:06:57.523 line:15%
that we were selling, so that we could sell more groceries,

153
00:06:58.550 --> 00:07:01.330 line:15%
we would have lost money rather than made.

154
00:07:01.330 --> 00:07:03.820 line:15%
So Donna said, well, you just teach the class.

155
00:07:03.820 --> 00:07:05.110
I said, but Donna, I'm not a chef.

156
00:07:05.110 --> 00:07:05.943
She said, who cares?

157
00:07:05.943 --> 00:07:07.260
Everybody likes your food.

158
00:07:07.260 --> 00:07:09.560
They buy it prepared in the case.

159
00:07:09.560 --> 00:07:11.410
So, you teach them.

160
00:07:11.410 --> 00:07:15.950
So the news station, WRGB, the world's first news station,

161
00:07:15.950 --> 00:07:20.450
because it's the home of GE, General Electric.

162
00:07:20.450 --> 00:07:23.090
It was literally the world's first television station.

163
00:07:23.090 --> 00:07:28.090
They came to do a piece on us and our little class.

164
00:07:28.310 --> 00:07:30.080
And it became a weekly segment,

165
00:07:30.080 --> 00:07:32.030
and I would go to people's homes,

166
00:07:32.030 --> 00:07:34.620
and firehouses, and police stations.

167
00:07:34.620 --> 00:07:37.590
And I would teach literally anybody

168
00:07:37.590 --> 00:07:41.140
who would let us in how to make a 30-minute meal.

169
00:07:41.140 --> 00:07:44.300
That's that, it was a weird circumstance.

170
00:07:44.300 --> 00:07:48.590
Well, I don't care about anyone's business prowess,

171
00:07:48.590 --> 00:07:51.850
because you can't deny the power of 30-Minute Meals.

172
00:07:51.850 --> 00:07:53.810
That's a billion-dollar idea.

173
00:07:53.810 --> 00:07:54.971
It's so good.

174
00:07:54.971 --> 00:07:58.388 line:15%
(energetic upbeat music)

175
00:07:59.400 --> 00:08:03.306 line:15%
♪ Oh-oh (Oh-oh) ♪

